{"cuisine/western-saharan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/western-saharan-cuisine","n":"Western Saharan cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of W. Sahara","intro":[["Western Saharan cuisine comprises the cuisine of Western Sahara, a disputed territory in the Maghreb region of North Africa, bordered by Morocco to the north, Algeria to the extreme northeast, Mauritania to the east and south, and the Atlantic Ocean to the west. The Western Saharan cuisine has several influences, as the population of that area, Sahrawis, are for the most part of Arabic and Berber origin. Saharawi cuisine is also influenced by Spanish cuisine owing to Spanish colonization."],["Food is primarily imported into Western Sahara, as minimal rainfall in the territory inhibits agricultural production. Indigenous sources of food include those derived from fishing and nomadic pastoralism. The labor and business in these indigenous provisions of foods are also a primary contributor of income for the territory's population, and are among the primary contributors to the economy of Western Sahara."],["A main staple food is the couscous that often accompanies one way or another all the food dishes. The influence of southern cuisine makes peanuts an accompaniment of some dishes."],["For meat, the Sahrawis favor camel and goat; lamb is also prominent. Some tribes are famous for growing wheat, barley and cereals in general."],["Some fruits and vegetables are grown in oases that are scattered within the territory."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"western-saharan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Saharan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Dining in Refugee Camps: The Art of Sahrawi Cooking","ref":"cookbook/dining-in-refugee-camps-the-art-of-sahrawi-cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Robin Kahn, 2010","note":"Artist's book recording the recipes and meals of Sahrawi women living in refugee camps.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781570272158","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Boulaghmane","dish/boulaghmane"]]}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/pitcairn-islands-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/pitcairn-islands-cuisine","n":"Pitcairn Islands cuisine","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Oceania","cuisines#oceania"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Pitcairn Is.","intro":[["The Pitcairn Islands, officially Pitcairn, Henderson, Ducie and Oeno Islands, are a group of four volcanic islands in the southern Pacific Ocean that form the sole British Overseas Territory in the Pacific Ocean. The four islands—Pitcairn, Henderson, Ducie and Oeno—are scattered across several hundred kilometres of ocean and have a combined land area of about 47 square kilometres (18 square miles). Henderson Island accounts for 86% of the land area, but only Pitcairn Island is inhabited. The inhabited islands nearest to the Pitcairn Islands are Mangareva (of French Polynesia), 688 km (428 miles) to the west, as well as Easter Island, 1,929 km (1199 miles) to the east."],["The Pitcairn Islanders are descended primarily from nine British HMS Bounty mutineers and twelve Tahitian women. In 2023, the territory had a permanent population of 35, making it the smallest territory in the world by number of permanent residents. Owing to the island's extreme isolation and small population, incidents of widespread sexual abuse went undetected until 1999, culminating in a high-profile sexual assault trial in 2004."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"pitcairn-islands-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pitcairn_Islands_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"Oceanic cuisine"},"cuisine/peasant-foods":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/peasant-foods","n":"Peasant foods","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Historical cuisine","intro":[["Peasant foods are dishes made from accessible and inexpensive ingredients, as would have been eaten by peasants."],["Studies on historical rural diets in Sweden indicates that peasant foods were predominantly grain-based. Periodic shortages in domestic grain production led to Sweden's reliance on imported grains while the Swedish peasants relied on locally available resources to survive."],["In many historical periods, peasant foods have been stigmatized."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peasant_foods"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Juls' Kitchen: Cucina povera","ref":"site/julskitchen-substack-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"julskitchen.substack.com","note":"Tuscan cook Giulia Scarpaleggia's newsletter post on the frugal Italian peasant cooking her cookbook covers.","url":"https://julskitchen.substack.com/p/cucina-povera-cookbook","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Cucina Povera: The Italian Way of Transforming Humble Ingredients into Unforgettable Meals","ref":"cookbook/cucina-povera-the-italian-way-of-transforming-humble-ingredients-into-unforgettable-meals","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Giulia Scarpaleggia","note":"Recipes from Italy's peasant tradition of cooking with bread, beans, greens and scraps.","url":"https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/giulia-scarpaleggia/cucina-povera/9781648290565/","suggested":true},{"name":"Cucina Povera: Tuscan Peasant Cooking","ref":"cookbook/cucina-povera-tuscan-peasant-cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Pamela Sheldon Johns","note":"Tuscan farmhouse recipes and stories gathered from rural cooks.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781449402389","suggested":true}]}],"cont":null},"ingredient/candied-fruit":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/candied-fruit","n":"Candied fruit","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 7 dishes across 8 cuisines","intro":[["Candied fruit, also known as glacé fruit, is whole fruit, smaller pieces of fruit, or pieces of peel, placed in heated sugar syrup, which absorbs the moisture from within the fruit and eventually preserves it. Depending on the size and type of fruit, this process can take from several days to several months. This process of preservation, which has been used since the 14th century, allows the fruit to remain edible for up to a year."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-candied-fruit"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candied_fruit"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":8,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Colomba pasquale","dish/colomba-pasquale"],["Pizza dolce di Beridde","dish/pizza-dolce-di-beridde"],["Spumoni","dish/spumoni"]]],["Swiss cuisine","cuisine/swiss-cuisine",[["Basler Läckerli","dish/basler-lackerli"],["Birnbrot","dish/birnbrot"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Calisson","dish/calisson"]]],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine",[["Stollen","dish/stollen"]]],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine",[["Pizza dolce di Beridde","dish/pizza-dolce-di-beridde"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Calisson","dish/calisson"]]],["Saxon cuisine","cuisine/saxon-cuisine",[["Stollen","dish/stollen"]]],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia",[["Stollen","dish/stollen"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Candied peel","ingredient/candied-peel"],["Hazelnut","ingredient/hazelnut"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Kirsch","ingredient/kirsch"],["Apple","ingredient/apple"],["Common fig","ingredient/common-fig"],["Pear","ingredient/pear"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Almond meal","ingredient/almond-meal"],["Fondant icing","ingredient/fondant-icing"],["Melon","ingredient/melon"]]}]},"ingredient/japanese-rice":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/japanese-rice","n":"Japanese rice","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Japanese rice refers to a number of short-grain cultivars of Japonica rice including ordinary rice (uruchimai) and glutinous rice (mochigome)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-japanese-rice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_rice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Yoshoku","cuisine/yoshoku",[["Chicken katsu","dish/chicken-katsu"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Fried chicken","ingredient/fried-chicken"],["Panko","ingredient/panko"]]}]},"ingredient/korean-fried-chicken":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/korean-fried-chicken","n":"Korean fried chicken","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Korean fried chicken, in Korean chikin, refers to a variety of fried chicken dishes created in South Korea. These include the basic huraideu-chicken (후라이드 치킨, from the English words 'fried chicken') and the spicy yangnyeom chicken (양념 치킨, 'seasoned chicken'). In South Korea, fried chicken is consumed as a meal, an appetizer, anju (food that is served and eaten with drinks), or as an after-meal snack."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-korean-fried-chicken"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_fried_chicken"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine",[["Chimaek","dish/chimaek"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beer","ingredient/beer"]]}]},"ingredient/lactuca":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/lactuca","n":"Lactuca","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Lactuca, commonly known as lettuce, is a genus of flowering plants in the family Asteraceae. The genus includes at 117 recognized species, distributed worldwide, but mainly in temperate Eurasia."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-lactuca"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lactuca"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Cobb salad","dish/cobb-salad"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Avocado","ingredient/avocado"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Blue cheese","ingredient/blue-cheese"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chicken breast","ingredient/chicken-breast"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Iceberg lettuce","ingredient/iceberg-lettuce"],["Romaine lettuce","ingredient/romaine-lettuce"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Vinaigrette","ingredient/vinaigrette"]]}]},"ingredient/ewe-s-milk":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ewe-s-milk","n":"Ewe's milk","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Sheep milk is the milk of domestic sheep. It is commonly used to make cultured dairy products, such as cheese. Some of the most popular sheep cheeses include feta (Greece), pecorino romano (Italy), Roquefort (France) and Manchego (Spain)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ewe-s-milk"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ewe's_milk"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Cuajada","dish/cuajada"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Raw milk","ingredient/raw-milk"],["Rennet","ingredient/rennet"]]}]},"ingredient/rose-water":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rose-water","n":"Rose water","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 7 cuisines","intro":[["Rose water or rosewater is a flavoured water created by steeping rose petals in water. It is typically made as a by-product during the distillation of rose petals to make rose oil for perfumes. Rose water is widely used to flavour culinary dishes and enhance cosmetic products, and it is significant in religious rituals throughout Eurasia. Iran is a major producer, supplying around 90% of the world's rose water demand."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rose-water"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_water"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine",[["Falooda","dish/falooda"],["Sholezard","dish/sholezard"]]],["Afghan cuisine","cuisine/afghan-cuisine",[["Falooda","dish/falooda"]]],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Falooda","dish/falooda"]]],["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine",[["Zalabiyeh","dish/zalabiyeh"]]],["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine",[["Falooda","dish/falooda"]]],["Palestinian cuisine","cuisine/palestinian-cuisine",[["Zalabiyeh","dish/zalabiyeh"]]],["Syrian cuisine","cuisine/syrian-cuisine",[["Halawet el jibn","dish/halawet-el-jibn"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Ocimum","ingredient/ocimum"],["Rose syrup","ingredient/rose-syrup"],["Sweet basil","ingredient/sweet-basil"],["Vermicelli","ingredient/vermicelli"],["Akkawi cheese","ingredient/akkawi-cheese"],["Pistachio","ingredient/pistachio"],["Qishta","ingredient/qishta"],["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Coffee","ingredient/coffee"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Saffron","ingredient/saffron"]]}]},"dish/flip-burger-boutique":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/flip-burger-boutique","n":"FLIP Burger Boutique","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["FLIP burger boutique (stylized as FLIP) was an upscale full-service American restaurant based in Atlanta, Georgia. The company opened its restaurant in West Midtown, Atlanta in 2008. The restaurant was generally well received by food critics for its ambiance and food, though there had been criticism that the restaurant was \"overdone\". It had been credited as increasing competition among hamburger restaurants in Atlanta."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-flip-burger-boutique"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FLIP_Burger_Boutique"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["5-8 Club","dish/5-8-club"],["Altabira City Tavern","dish/altabira-city-tavern"],["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Applebee's","dish/applebee-s"],["Arby's","dish/arby-s"],["Arnold's Bar and Grill","dish/arnold-s-bar-and-grill"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Be Our Guest Restaurant","dish/be-our-guest-restaurant"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Ben's Chili Bowl","dish/ben-s-chili-bowl"],["Benjy's","dish/benjy-s"],["Besaw's","dish/besaw-s"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blackhawk (restaurant)","dish/blackhawk-restaurant"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Bobcat Bite","dish/bobcat-bite"],["Boiling Springs Tavern","dish/boiling-springs-tavern"],["Brix Tavern","dish/brix-tavern"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brown Derby","dish/brown-derby"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Buona","dish/buona"],["Busboys and Poets","dish/busboys-and-poets"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Byways Cafe","dish/byways-cafe"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["California Grill","dish/california-grill"],["Camp Washington Chili","dish/camp-washington-chili"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Carnation Cafe","dish/carnation-cafe"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"]]}]},"ingredient/frangipane":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/frangipane","n":"Frangipane","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Frangipane ( FRAN-jih-pa(y)n) is a sweet almond-flavoured cream typical in French patisserie. Frangipane is used in a variety of ways, including cakes and such pastries as the Bakewell tart, conversation tart, Jésuite, and galette des rois. The first written record was in a French cookbook from 1674, where it was spelt franchipane, and was described as a custard tart flavoured with pounded almonds and pistachios. However, food historian Claudine Brecourt-Villars, writing in 1996, says, in her view, frangipane meant cream. Alan Davidson, in his book The Oxford Companion to Food, called Frangipane an almond based mixture."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-frangipane"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frangipane"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Argentine cuisine","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",[["Jésuite","dish/jesuite"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Pithivier","dish/pithivier"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Pithivier","dish/pithivier"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Puff pastry","ingredient/puff-pastry"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Powdered sugar","ingredient/powdered-sugar"],["Almond paste","ingredient/almond-paste"]]}]},"dish/mao-xue-wang":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/mao-xue-wang","n":"Mao xue wang","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Chinese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Mao xue wang is a Chongqing cuisine dish from Chongqing, China. The dish consists of blood curds and tripe along with mung bean sprouts, served in a  mala seasoned oily broth. While similar to Chongqing hot pot in that the broth is not intended to be eaten, mao xue wang is served fully cooked in a communal bowl and is not simmered at the table, comparable to shuizhu."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-mao-xue-wang"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mao_xue_wang"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Blood curd","ingredient/blood-curd"],["Chili oil","ingredient/chili-oil"],["Mala (seasoning)","ingredient/mala-seasoning"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Woks of Life","ref":"site/thewoksoflife-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thewoksoflife.com","note":"Chinese and Chinese American recipes from the Leung family, running since 2013.","url":"https://thewoksoflife.com/","suggested":true},{"name":"Chinese Cooking Demystified","ref":"site/youtube-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"youtube.com","note":"Technique-heavy YouTube channel on regional Chinese home and restaurant cooking.","url":"https://www.youtube.com/@ChineseCookingDemystified","suggested":true},{"name":"Red House Spice","ref":"site/redhousespice-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"redhousespice.com","note":"Classic Chinese recipes from Wei Guo, who grew up in Gansu, blogging since 2017.","url":"https://redhousespice.com/about/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Chinese cuisine","links":[["A+ Hong Kong Kitchen","dish/a-hong-kong-kitchen"],["Almond biscuit","dish/almond-biscuit"],["Banana roll","dish/banana-roll"],["Baozi","dish/baozi"],["Bear paw","dish/bear-paw"],["Black sesame roll","dish/black-sesame-roll"],["Century egg","dish/century-egg"],["Cha siu bao